00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:38.527
something we're thinking of.

00:17:38.527 --> 00:17:42.190
I think generally
in terms of the MMM,

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:44.770
this is a project that I kicked
off with a data scientist

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:46.020
that I hired back in November.

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.100
So we're still pretty
early on building out

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.620
our initial proof of
concept for a single outcome

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:52.720
variable for a single brand.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.850
I think as we continue
to expand on this one

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:57.520
and get a lot more
iterative and bring out

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:00.730
a bigger scale to a global model
and also on different outcome

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.530
variables, definitely
awareness is

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:04.087
one that's top of mind for us.

00:18:04.087 --> 00:18:05.920
So we have a significant
amount of marketing

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.590
spend right now that's focused
on really capturing user

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:10.780
intent-- that they're
already in the market,

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:12.530
and they're searching
for something that's

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:14.020
very relevant to our company.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.880
But considering a
lot more how do we

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:17.980
start scaling to
truly drive awareness

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:21.220
across those engaged or
pre-engaged couples who

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:24.190
will be engaged soon so that
we're already top of mind

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:26.440
when they get to that stage
rather than after the fact

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:27.340
and find us after.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.160
It's pretty significant
benefit in the US

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:34.120
that the Knot has really
strong brand awareness already.

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:35.740
But it definitely
could be better.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.200
Wedding Wire, our
other brand in the US,

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:40.420
is still strong but not
quite as strong as that Knot.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:41.980
So it's definitely
one of the things

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:45.410
we're always considering.

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:48.620
Something I know
we were talking

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:53.150
about a little bit earlier in
preparing for this week is you

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:55.400
talked a little bit about
how you drive and stir

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.310
empirical thinking with
your teams and marketing

00:18:58.310 --> 00:18:59.240
stakeholders.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:02.420
And I remember googling, like,
what that actually meant.

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:03.590
And now I don't remember.

00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:06.540
So, can you tell me a
little bit about that?

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:09.212
Yeah, so I was working--

00:19:09.212 --> 00:19:11.420
so when I was working at
Domino's-- this was probably

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.190
back in 2018--

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:16.760
I went to this conference called
ARF for Advertising Research

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:18.830
Foundation, and it's
just a conference

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:21.200
for people that work in
advertising and marketing

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:24.470
space, whether that's
advertisers themselves or brand

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:27.560
marketers or data scientists,
to get together to kind of talk

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:28.700
through best practices.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:34.100
And the keynote in that
conference, the speaker

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.980
said, I want you all to
take away one thing today,

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:39.380
and that's to go back to
your workplace and foment

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:40.400
empiricism.

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.930
And at the time, I didn't really
understand what that meant.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:45.530
And then as I continued to kind
of sit on that a little bit

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:47.900
and let it marinate, I
really thought about it and--

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:51.990
because generally, foment has
a very negative connotation,

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:54.500
which is kind of like, I'm
going to stir up and cause

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:55.830
some trouble.

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:58.370
But if you consider
fomenting empiricism,

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:01.610
it's like, within whatever
workplace you're in

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:04.250
or whatever setting
you're in, just driving

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:08.300
the thinking of having
data and empirical evidence

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.400
to support your decision
making, I think it really

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:12.440
goes a long way for, as
I'm talking to my team

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.300
or talking to our
business stakeholders,

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:18.620
to think through we may have
a gut feeling about something,

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:21.260
but how can we think a little
more analytically minded

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:24.350
and try to challenge ourselves
to see what the data would show

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:27.800
us and try to drive a bit
more value in terms of what's

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.050
capable there rather than
kind of just taking things

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:32.365
status quo?

00:20:32.365 --> 00:20:34.240
I'm guessing that's what
you're referring to.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:35.550
Yes, that is.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:38.520
But I'm curious if
there's like a time that

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:42.720
comes to mind that your team
did that or project where

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:45.000
that helped the outcome.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.790
Yeah, I think generally,
it's more of a mentality

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.880
that I try to push with my team,
especially as we get requests

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.410
from stakeholders.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:56.560
So, one thing is I try to
push an embedded nature,

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:58.933
especially within
the Analytics team.

00:20:58.933 --> 00:21:01.350
The data scientist team are
more focused on like a project

00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:01.850
basis.

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:05.850
The analysts are really
embedded within the teams

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:06.850
that we're working with.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:09.810
So this could range from brand
media, social media, content,

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.280
SEO, CRM, performance marketing.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.530
We work with a lot of different
marketing departments.

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:15.900
And the analysts are
basically aligned

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.760
to some of these
different aspects

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:21.180
to really be embedded within
those cultures and not just

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:23.700
an outsider kind of
looking in to try

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:26.640
to get context after the fact
when they have a question.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.950
So, as they're
embedded, it allows

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:33.570
for us to think as a team rather
than say, hey, I'm a marketer.

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:34.410
Hey, data team.

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:35.280
I have this problem.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:36.300
Can you solve it for me?

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:40.770
It's more this is a problem
that we have as a company.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:43.950
Now, how can we think
together to better ideate

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:45.460
this overall business problem?

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:46.918
So, the analysts
really have a seat

00:21:46.918 --> 00:21:50.280
at the table to have
a lot of the ideation

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:52.290
to identify that we even
have a business problem

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:55.410
to begin with, and then
take that business problem

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.990
and translate it now
ourselves as the data team

00:21:57.990 --> 00:22:00.030
into a data problem
and then build a data

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:01.477
solution to solve that problem.

00:22:01.477 --> 00:22:03.060
But I think a lot
of it is just coming

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:07.110
back to having this mindset of
making sure that we're keeping

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:09.720
focused on the end goal that
we're looking for, and not

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:11.670
necessarily say, hey,
data team, can you

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:12.960
go pull this data for us?

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.798
Like, the gut reaction is, sure,
I can easily do that for you.

00:22:15.798 --> 00:22:18.090
But like, what's the intent
of the question that you're

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:20.548
trying to solve or the problem
that you're trying to solve?

00:22:20.548 --> 00:22:22.230
And let us think a
bit more holistically

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.150
and as a partnership
so that we can

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:27.690
think to try to drive a bit
more solutioning and decisioning

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:30.090
behind that because
we as data science

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:32.340
and analytics already
have that mindset

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:34.560
and know a bit more
of what data can do.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.350
The stakeholders,
even though they do--

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:39.210
they are very focused
in, like, having

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:42.673
data drive their organization,
they don't necessarily

00:22:42.673 --> 00:22:43.590
know what data can do.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.507
They know how they would
want to analyze data,

00:22:45.507 --> 00:22:47.160
but it's not necessarily
the right way.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:49.320
So, to kind of come back
to your question, Rachel,

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:52.710
about a specific instance, we
received one question early

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:56.620
on from one of our
stakeholders when I came here,

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:59.820
and it was, hey, can you go
do a segmentation for us,

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:02.580
or can you build
some clusters for us?

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.380
And when it's like--

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:05.858
I mean, yeah, we could.

00:23:05.858 --> 00:23:07.650
Like, what are the
hypotheses that you have

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:08.500
that you're trying to solve?

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.970
What is the business problem
you're trying to solve?

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:11.520
And I didn't really
have the context

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:13.437
of the business at the
time because I was only

00:23:13.437 --> 00:23:14.310
about a month in.

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:15.960
But it really comes
back to trying

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:18.630
to identify what is it you're
trying to solve out of this.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.520
If I deliver some
segments for you,

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:22.590
how are you going to use
that to drive decisions?

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:24.360
Are you going to
have personalization?

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:26.507
Is it going to change
your audience strategy?

00:23:26.507 --> 00:23:28.590
Is it going to allow us
to do some type of testing

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:30.270
to make sure they're relevant?

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.520
So I think just continuing
to have those conversations

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:35.640
with stakeholders allows it to
be a bit more focused and less

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:37.620
prescriptive from their
part and more so, like,

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:40.530
thinking a bit more generally
about what can be done.

00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:47.210
We're actually just talking
about that a bit internally

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:50.750
this week and thinking
about the problem

00:23:50.750 --> 00:23:52.610
first before we start
jumping into all

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:55.160
these different solutions.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:57.800
But part of that was also,
like, getting everybody

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:01.010
to agree on what the problem is.

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:04.400
Is that something you have
to do across all teams?

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:07.490
JOSH KING: Yeah, it's definitely
something we have to do,

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:10.700
and I think it's a bit
easier when you take it back

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:12.890
in terms of
prioritization because we

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:16.640
kind of as a company what our
goal is over the next five

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:17.810
years.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:20.060
And we all-- as we're doing
our own objective setting,

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:22.440
we're making sure that they
kind of align up to that.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:27.470
So if we receive
a question about,

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:29.840
hey, you can we do
this analysis to see

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:32.780
what's going to happen
here, whether that's

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:35.210
like an analysis for which
marketing campaign worked

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:39.020
better or which specific
channel on a specific campaign

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:44.060
drove the most xyz
aspect within our brand,

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:47.720
it kind of comes down to how
is this going to help us align

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:50.695
towards us reaching our
goal because there's

00:24:50.695 --> 00:24:52.070
a significant
amount of questions

00:24:52.070 --> 00:24:53.903
that we ourselves are
asking ourselves, that

00:24:53.903 --> 00:24:55.580
are our marketing
team is asking,

00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:59.038
that our product team is asking,
our operations team is asking.

00:24:59.038 --> 00:25:00.830
And there's a lot more
questions than there

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:02.160
are resources within the team.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:03.650
So it really comes
down to having

00:25:03.650 --> 00:25:06.770
a very level head in terms of
prioritization to make sure

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.180
that we're focused on
the right problems.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.780
And then if we
can't align on there

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:15.440
being a problem
in itself, then I

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.570
think that's one of those
general things where it's

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:20.100
let's kind of think through
what are some of our hypotheses

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:22.550
and then continue to refine
a bit about what it is we're

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:24.260
trying to get out of this.

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:26.750
Sometimes, that's just relevant
for an exploration exercise

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.280
to say, do we really
have a problem,

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:31.640
or do we kind of
just think there is/

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:32.405
Thank you.

00:25:32.405 --> 00:25:34.430
Ahnas, I see you
have your hand raise.

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:36.850
Do you want to jump in here?

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:38.100
Sure.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:40.410
Josh, so I want to
pick your brain on--

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:44.040
I'm assuming that your company
does use social media data

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.890
to find prospective customers.

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:49.560
And I'm just curious of--

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:51.810
obviously, whatever you
can share-- if you use R,

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:55.690
if you use any other
tools in that process,

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:59.098
whether to feed it
into a model or--

00:25:59.098 --> 00:25:59.640
I don't know.

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:01.560
That's a personal
interest of mine,

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:05.590
leveraging social media data and
incorporating it into a model.

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:09.710
JOSH KING: Yeah,
so I think there's

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:11.210
a couple of different
aspects there.

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:17.090
One is generally, within the
newer privacy constraints,

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:21.230
it's hard to do any type of
user level analysis where

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:23.330
you necessarily know
specifically, especially

00:26:23.330 --> 00:26:25.993
within social media, any
actions that a user is taking

00:26:25.993 --> 00:26:27.410
and then align--
especially if you

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:28.993
want to align that
to something that's

00:26:28.993 --> 00:26:30.750
happening within our platform.

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:31.790
So that's one aspect.

00:26:31.790 --> 00:26:36.170
As an aside, I'm somewhat
generally for sharing my data

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:39.860
because as a data scientist
and as a marketing focus data

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:42.680
scientist, I'd
prefer to have ads

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:44.982
that are 100% relevant to me
so that I don't have to go

00:26:44.982 --> 00:26:46.190
searching for something else.

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:48.565
I just like-- every single ad
that I receive is something

00:26:48.565 --> 00:26:51.440
that I want or would need.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:53.670
So, that's an aside.

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:56.600
The other aspect there
is getting into, like,

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:58.820
using social media data.

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:02.775
So, one specific aspect that
you could utilize there,

00:27:02.775 --> 00:27:05.150
and this is, like, under the
assumption that maybe you're

00:27:05.150 --> 00:27:08.840
working for a brand or you
have some means of aligning

00:27:08.840 --> 00:27:14.210
on driving relevant people to
whatever platform you may have,

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:18.590
is by using signals that
you can send back to-- so,

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:20.720
things like Google or
Facebook-- that allow them

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:25.940
on their end to do some type
of lookalike audience building

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:29.990
where it's like, hey,
I've received these 1,000

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:31.640
people from your
platform, and here's

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:33.680
the click that they
came into our site on.

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:35.490
I don't necessarily
know who they are.

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.600
But I can say, like,
this is the value that we

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:41.640
see based on some
predictive model we've built

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.970
and then send that value back
to those social media channels

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:46.820
so that they can
therefore say, OK,

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:48.510
I know how much you
spend on this click.

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:50.260
I know the value that
you receive from it.

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:52.130
Now I can help optimize
the return on that

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:54.770
spend that you're giving us send
more relevant customers back

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:57.218
And they can do that via
lookalike audience modeling.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:00.890
Cool.

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:01.970
Thank you.

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:04.170
JOSH KING: Yeah, sure.

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:08.203
There was actually a follow up
question that was asked-- well,

00:28:08.203 --> 00:28:09.120
maybe not a follow up.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:10.980
It was asked
anonymously earlier,

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:16.490
but have the recent privacy
changes and browsers affected

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:19.970
your MTA modeling
if you do this,

00:28:19.970 --> 00:28:22.970
and I might need to
know what MTA is,

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:26.165
and push your
efforts towards MMM?

00:28:26.165 --> 00:28:29.118
JOSH KING: Yeah, so, MTA stands
for multitouch attribution--

00:28:29.118 --> 00:28:29.910
RACHEL DEMPSEY: OK.

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:32.630
--which is a means of
aligning all of the marketing

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:35.630
touchpoints that a user could
have before they make some

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:37.440
action or they don't
make an action.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.800
And so, yes, definitely
privacy constraints

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:41.150
are a significant blocker there.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:44.090
Even thinking back to, like,
2018 when we were doing this

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:46.730
at Domino's, there was
a significant blocker

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:48.650
within the social media
channels specifically

00:28:48.650 --> 00:28:49.858
that are very walled gardens.

00:28:49.858 --> 00:28:51.920
You can't align on
an impression level

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.660
basis who's seeing your ads.

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:55.520
So it's definitely
a challenge there.

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:57.980
One way that we're
trying to work around

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:00.590
that is by, rather
than building out

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:02.840
peer what I would call
MTA, which is generally

00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:06.140
using some type of Markov
chain model, that is impression

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:09.050
level, that we're building out
more of a click-based MTA which

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:11.210
aligns for a specific user.

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:13.640
Again, this kind of comes down
to our identity resolution

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:16.160
and how well we can
align any specific click

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:17.950
to any specific individual.

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:19.700
For any of the clicks
that they're making,

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:21.117
what is the
allocation that we may

00:29:21.117 --> 00:29:23.990
give to each of the marketing
touchpoints that they have

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:25.500
before they make some action?

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:28.440
So, that allows us to get
a bit more perspective

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:30.740
into some what you may
call supporting marketing

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:33.920
channels rather than, say, the
last click before an action

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:36.240
happens or the first click
before an action happens.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:38.823
So you can kind of consider some
different weighting scenarios

00:29:38.823 --> 00:29:40.070
there that may do it.

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:42.470
The other aspect there
was getting into the MMM,

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:46.130
and this is kind of, like I'd
say, over the last four or five

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.470
years, there's been a bit
of a resurgence in MMM

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:51.410
where prior to that, everybody
was saying, oh, MMM is dead.

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:54.020
It's such an archaic
method that's

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:55.820
being utilized to
optimize marketing spend.

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:57.880
And yeah, it was
developed in the '60s,

00:29:57.880 --> 00:29:59.630
but it's still highly
relevant, especially

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:01.920
now as privacy concerns
are coming out,

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:05.360
especially with in Europe, where
privacy laws are already well

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:07.100
ahead what they are in the US.

00:30:07.100 --> 00:30:09.740
So, the benefit to an MMM
is that it doesn't require

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.090
user level data to do this.

00:30:11.090 --> 00:30:14.030
It's very looking at it from
an econometrics perspective

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:16.730
and building out a model
that can be very focused

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:19.430
across the entirety and not just
within digital channels-- also

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:22.817
within TV and terrestrial
radio and billboards--

00:30:22.817 --> 00:30:25.400
to get a read into how all the
marketing channels kind of work

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:25.900
together.

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:30.000
Thank you.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.770
Martin, I see you
have your hand raised.

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:33.400
Nice to see you.

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:34.530
Nice to see you, Rachel.

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:35.400
Hi, I'm Martin.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.340
I am the CMO at Union.ai.

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:43.410
We do data, and
ML orchestrators.

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:48.590
I used to work for RStudio,
So I know some people here.

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:50.350
So, I think this is
super interesting.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:51.892
I asked the last
question [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:51.892 --> 00:30:55.410
MTA versus [INAUDIBLE] Markov
chain-based MTAs and so on so.

00:30:55.410 --> 00:31:00.900
But I see also the resurgence of
MMM, but it has issues, right?

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:03.120
I mean, if you have
time series that

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:07.290
are short or spasticity
of observations,

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:08.620
it's not going to be good.

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.210
So, how do you overcome that?

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:12.660
Yeah, that's definitely a
challenge have to kind of work

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:15.270
with.

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:17.760
One potential method
which, like, granted,

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:20.400
it's not necessarily going
to always be relevant.

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:22.938
But if you're able to go
further in granularity,

00:31:22.938 --> 00:31:25.230
then that can start to improve
some of the sample sizes

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:27.720
you have available in
order to build out a model

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:30.720
and allows you to do a bit
more robust techniques.

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:35.100
So right now, the model
that we're building is, one,

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:38.018
it's using open source framework
from Facebook called Robin,

00:31:38.018 --> 00:31:39.060
which is pretty powerful.

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:42.330
And it's built in R, and
it's pretty out of the box

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:45.450
capable of doing
pretty robust MMMs.

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:48.000
Next steps beyond that
are getting into more--

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:49.530
when you think of,
like, rigorously

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:52.230
focused ones, getting into
hierarchical time series,

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:54.480
using Bayesian
methods, which, there's

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:56.550
a package in R
called RStan, which

00:31:56.550 --> 00:31:59.760
uses the Stan framework to
get into that Bayesian level.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:01.290
That's what we used at Domino's.

00:32:01.290 --> 00:32:04.110
And within that, as you
continue to increase

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:07.380
the levels of granularity,
you can use the posterior

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:11.160
distribution from one model
to inform the priors getting

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:13.380
into another one, which
allows you to account

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:17.140
for some of that
sparsity you may have

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.390
just by getting through there.

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:21.760
Another means is by if you
don't want to go further

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:23.760
in granularity, you can
actually do the opposite

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:26.310
and start to aggregate some
of those marketing channels

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:28.600
that may be relevant from
a consumer's perspective.

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:31.100
So, one of the considerations
we made with the current model

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:34.050
that we're focusing
on at TKWW is

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:36.818
by aggregating
Pinterest and Instagram,

00:32:36.818 --> 00:32:39.360
which, like, they're completely
different marketing channels.

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:42.840
And they have-- Meta
and Pinterest have

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:45.270
no combination to each other.

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.058
But from a consumer
perspective, when

00:32:47.058 --> 00:32:48.600
they're considering
wedding planning,

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:51.270
they're both used as a means
of getting visual inspiration

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:53.400
for planning their wedding
and seeing photos that

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:55.020
are relevant to search for.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:57.750
So it makes sense to aggregate
both of those together because

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:00.190
of the sparsity that we had
in some of those channels,

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:03.307
which allows us to get a good
insight to say we may not

00:33:03.307 --> 00:33:04.890
be able to see what
Pinterest is doing

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:06.510
or what Instagram
is doing in a silo,

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:08.130
but we can say for
visual inspiration,

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:09.900
it has this return
on our ad spend.

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:13.560
And then as we get into
further models later on,

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:16.368
then you can get into
that hierarchical analysis

00:33:16.368 --> 00:33:17.910
where are you able
to then parse down

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:19.560
what's actually happening
within each of those channels

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:20.400
specifically.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:25.692
Thanks.

00:33:25.692 --> 00:33:28.468
Caitlin, I see you had a
question in the chat a bit

00:33:28.468 --> 00:33:30.135
earlier too if you
want to jump in next.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:38.130
And I can get started
with the question,

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:40.620
but it was, how
do you collaborate

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:42.060
between R and Python?

00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:44.830
Are these separate teams?

00:33:44.830 --> 00:33:46.570
They're not separate teams.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:50.200
It kind of depends on what
people are used to using,

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:52.960
and that's definitely a struggle
to consider when you want

00:33:52.960 --> 00:33:58.450
to do quality assurance on the
code basis that's being written

00:33:58.450 --> 00:34:01.210
if one of the data scientists
is an expert in the problem

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:04.000
that you're trying to solve or
has some knowledge about it,

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:06.970
but they know Python, and I'm
coding in R. Then definitely

00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:08.560
becomes a challenge.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:12.340
We're currently working on
building out our overall data

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:14.320
roadmap for our
department on, like,

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:16.690
what is the appropriate
means and scenario here

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:19.810
for getting it and
being able to have

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:23.360
these cross-code capabilities.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:27.949
I think one of the
challenges there is again,

00:34:27.949 --> 00:34:29.659
if you're focused on
a specific language,

00:34:29.659 --> 00:34:31.760
you're not necessarily
focused on the problem

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:36.447
itself, which you can also
relate to if you're focused on,

00:34:36.447 --> 00:34:38.239
hey, I just found this
new novel technique,

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:40.364
let me see how I can
pigeonhole it into the problem

00:34:40.364 --> 00:34:42.860
that I have, then you're not
really focused on the problem

00:34:42.860 --> 00:34:45.179
because that may
not be as relevant.

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:47.270
So I think one of
the areas to do

00:34:47.270 --> 00:34:50.750
is just have some
means of identifying

00:34:50.750 --> 00:34:53.929
who would be relevant to
look at your codebase,

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:56.030
even if they're not
necessarily on the same team.

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:58.700
I've been at some
companies in the past that

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:01.790
were significant in size where
there was, like, basically

00:35:01.790 --> 00:35:04.590
an internal R user group and
an internal Python user group,

00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:08.420
which allows for a lot of that
language-specific collaboration

00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:09.290
and discussions.

00:35:12.250 --> 00:35:14.540
Thank you.

