00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:03.640
So--

00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:08.450
Thank you.

00:49:08.450 --> 00:49:12.200
A question that I
always like to ask Josh

00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:15.140
is, is there a piece
of career advice

00:49:15.140 --> 00:49:18.680
that you've ever received
or maybe that you've

00:49:18.680 --> 00:49:22.430
given that stands out to you?

00:49:22.430 --> 00:49:25.460
Ooh, that's a good one.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:27.560
Well, I kind of actually
already used one of them,

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:29.470
which was if you're in
a meeting and you're

00:49:29.470 --> 00:49:33.610
asked if you have any
questions, then ask a question.

00:49:33.610 --> 00:49:36.215
But I also--

00:49:36.215 --> 00:49:37.840
I feel like I can't
repurpose that one,

00:49:37.840 --> 00:49:39.910
but maybe I could punt
and say that I would.

00:49:39.910 --> 00:49:41.950
But I think there
is something that's

00:49:41.950 --> 00:49:45.220
somewhat relevant to that even
though it's not necessarily

00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:50.140
a piece of advice but
just a general mannerism.

00:49:50.140 --> 00:49:55.330
If you're giving a
presentation and you get to end

00:49:55.330 --> 00:49:57.660
and you say, do you
have any questions,

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:00.550
it's much easier to get
some engagement from others

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:03.430
if you reframe that to say,
what questions do you have?

00:50:03.430 --> 00:50:05.650
I think, like, generally,
and I've seen this also

00:50:05.650 --> 00:50:07.180
from experience-- granted,
I didn't set it up

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:07.840
as an A/B test.

00:50:07.840 --> 00:50:11.740
But you get a lot more
relevance coming out of it

00:50:11.740 --> 00:50:13.990
and engagement if you say,
what questions do you have,

00:50:13.990 --> 00:50:15.850
and people are more
likely to do that.

00:50:15.850 --> 00:50:19.240
Also, if you ask
somebody what questions

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:21.250
do you have or do you
have any questions,

00:50:21.250 --> 00:50:23.030
count to 17 in your head.

00:50:23.030 --> 00:50:25.570
It's not like 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:50:25.570 --> 00:50:28.240
It's like 1, 2, 3, 4,
5, 6, getting to 17.

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:31.990
And that pause is
sufficient enough

00:50:31.990 --> 00:50:35.288
to allow somebody to kind of
wrap their thoughts together

00:50:35.288 --> 00:50:37.330
and build out something
so that they can actually

00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:38.750
have something ready for you.

00:50:38.750 --> 00:50:41.292
A lot of times, somebody will
say, do you have any questions?

00:50:41.292 --> 00:50:43.090
OK, good, and then
see you later.

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:45.460
But actually having that
specific pause in there

00:50:45.460 --> 00:50:48.040
and actually doing that
mental counting in your head

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:50.950
allows for a decent amount of
time to actually get there.

00:50:53.810 --> 00:50:56.660
The thought of waiting for
17 seconds there just gave me

00:50:56.660 --> 00:51:00.104
a little bit of anxiety, but--

00:51:00.104 --> 00:51:01.610
I've called it
out specifically--

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:02.910
Are there any questions?

00:51:02.910 --> 00:51:05.077
Yeah, I've called that
out specifically to my team

00:51:05.077 --> 00:51:07.520
so that if they feel a
very long pause from me,

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:09.253
that's exactly what
I'm doing because I

00:51:09.253 --> 00:51:10.920
heard this piece of
advice at one point.

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:14.450
And so it's not that I'm just,
like, sitting there waiting--

00:51:14.450 --> 00:51:15.920
I'm waiting indefinitely.

00:51:15.920 --> 00:51:17.525
It's just I have
this specific pause.

00:51:17.525 --> 00:51:20.150
KATHERINE GERTON: Yeah, and with
Zoom, it's not like, oh, maybe

00:51:20.150 --> 00:51:22.083
he's having audio difficulty.

00:51:22.083 --> 00:51:23.000
RACHEL DEMPSEY: Right.

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:23.500
Exactly.

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:28.460
So, actually, are
there any questions

00:51:28.460 --> 00:51:33.010
that I have missed in
the Zoom chat here?

00:51:33.010 --> 00:51:35.260
Feel free to just raise your
hand if there's something

00:51:35.260 --> 00:51:38.500
that you want to bring to the
top or something we've missed.

00:51:38.500 --> 00:51:41.260
I actually did just see
one come in on Slido

00:51:41.260 --> 00:51:44.560
right now that was
what important roles

00:51:44.560 --> 00:51:47.740
or opportunities have you taken
throughout your career that has

00:51:47.740 --> 00:51:50.588
helped shape who you are today.

00:51:50.588 --> 00:51:52.130
Oh, that's a really
great question.

00:51:52.130 --> 00:51:54.550
So I think there was one.

00:51:54.550 --> 00:52:00.730
So I used to work at Domino's,
and I was promoted to Director,

00:52:00.730 --> 00:52:03.545
and then I think, like,
six months later--

00:52:03.545 --> 00:52:05.920
so, my wife was a professor
at the University of Michigan

00:52:05.920 --> 00:52:07.060
at that point.

00:52:07.060 --> 00:52:08.650
But we're both
from the Carolinas.

00:52:08.650 --> 00:52:10.840
And Duke University
reached out to her

00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:12.820
and ended up offering
her a position.

00:52:12.820 --> 00:52:15.640
And so we were moving
back to the Carolinas

00:52:15.640 --> 00:52:17.260
to be closer to family.

00:52:17.260 --> 00:52:20.110
And then I wasn't really
finding the right job

00:52:20.110 --> 00:52:23.530
in an industry that I
felt really strong about.

00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:26.590
But I had-- as I was just kind
of talking to people that I was

00:52:26.590 --> 00:52:30.940
meeting through my wife's
academic relationships,

00:52:30.940 --> 00:52:32.500
I met these--

00:52:32.500 --> 00:52:34.630
at Duke, they had a
new program called

00:52:34.630 --> 00:52:37.420
Master and Interdisciplinary
Data Science.

00:52:37.420 --> 00:52:40.565
And within that one, I really
loved kind of the framing

00:52:40.565 --> 00:52:41.440
that they were doing.

00:52:41.440 --> 00:52:42.857
And they were
looking for somebody

00:52:42.857 --> 00:52:45.340
who had a lot of
industry experience

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:49.120
to come in and be
faculty to not just talk

00:52:49.120 --> 00:52:52.480
about the theoretics behind the
machine learning applications

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:54.670
that are happening, which,
like, at Duke, there's

00:52:54.670 --> 00:52:57.280
a lot of amazing professors that
can help drive a lot of that,

00:52:57.280 --> 00:53:00.550
but they're looking for somebody
that had a lot of that softer

00:53:00.550 --> 00:53:02.770
skill aspect that you can
find with a strong data

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:04.870
scientist, the work
through project management

00:53:04.870 --> 00:53:07.840
and build relationships
and ask the right questions

00:53:07.840 --> 00:53:10.270
and effective
storytelling, et cetera.

00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:13.120
So, because I wasn't finding
the right job in industry,

00:53:13.120 --> 00:53:14.500
I wanted to go with that one.

00:53:14.500 --> 00:53:18.538
And sadly, my wife was like, oh,
you're going to hate academia.

00:53:18.538 --> 00:53:20.080
It's not like working
in an industry.

00:53:20.080 --> 00:53:22.010
I'm like, no, no, it's
going to be great.

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:25.750
And then I got in there, and
it was very changing for me.

00:53:25.750 --> 00:53:27.430
I saw a lot of
different perspectives

00:53:27.430 --> 00:53:31.570
and was able to get a lot of
different project experience

00:53:31.570 --> 00:53:32.690
as I was doing that.

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:37.888
I did find pretty quickly that
academia is just not for me,

00:53:37.888 --> 00:53:40.180
but like I still feel like
it was something that really

00:53:40.180 --> 00:53:41.680
changed me for the
better as I was working

00:53:41.680 --> 00:53:44.050
with a lot of these grad
students that were really early

00:53:44.050 --> 00:53:45.310
on in building their
career and seeing

00:53:45.310 --> 00:53:46.810
a lot of these different
projects that were being worked

00:53:46.810 --> 00:53:47.310
on.

00:53:50.150 --> 00:53:51.770
Thank you.

00:53:51.770 --> 00:53:54.830
I do see one other question that
came in the chat from Jonathan,

00:53:54.830 --> 00:53:57.290
and Jonathan said,
for someone that's

00:53:57.290 --> 00:54:00.380
new to the field of data
science and has experience using

00:54:00.380 --> 00:54:05.460
R and Python with banking, what
are helpful resources, books,

00:54:05.460 --> 00:54:08.000
or communities you
recommend that could support

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:10.730
with that transition
to data science?

00:54:10.730 --> 00:54:12.950
Yeah, so my
master's was actually

00:54:12.950 --> 00:54:15.660
in computational finance.

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:18.830
So it's something that I have
had a bit of focus on it,

00:54:18.830 --> 00:54:22.190
but a lot of it wasn't,
like, the actual mathematics

00:54:22.190 --> 00:54:29.690
side-- like, building out some
pretty intense math theorems

00:54:29.690 --> 00:54:31.790
for them and stuff like that.

00:54:31.790 --> 00:54:35.870
But one of the books that
I go to even still now is--

00:54:35.870 --> 00:54:38.640
and I can follow up after
and send this to you.

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:43.480
But it's like a an advanced
econometrics using R book.

00:54:43.480 --> 00:54:45.230
And I think that's one
that I go to a lot,

00:54:45.230 --> 00:54:48.155
and econometrics is very
heavily used within finance.

00:54:48.155 --> 00:54:50.780
That's getting into a lot of the
different types of time series

00:54:50.780 --> 00:54:51.980
analyses.

00:54:51.980 --> 00:54:53.720
I wouldn't have as
much recommendations

00:54:53.720 --> 00:54:55.762
if you're trying to get
into algorithmic trading.

00:54:55.762 --> 00:54:57.980
I had a class in that
one, but it wasn't

00:54:57.980 --> 00:54:59.090
highly interesting to me.

00:54:59.090 --> 00:55:01.293
It's an amazingly
interesting subject but not

00:55:01.293 --> 00:55:02.960
one of those ones
that I'm an expert in.

00:55:07.050 --> 00:55:08.080
Thank you very much.

00:55:08.080 --> 00:55:11.550
I was just asking in the chat
if anybody can find that book,

00:55:11.550 --> 00:55:13.425
feel free to put it
there in the chat.

00:55:13.425 --> 00:55:15.910
I was trying to do
Google at the same time.

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:16.410
Hey, Trevor.

00:55:16.410 --> 00:55:17.160
I see your hand.

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:19.570
--Advanced Micro Econometrics
or something like that,

00:55:19.570 --> 00:55:21.980
and it was all in R.

00:55:21.980 --> 00:55:23.122
Awesome.

00:55:23.122 --> 00:55:24.580
Hey, Trevor I see
your hand raised.

00:55:24.580 --> 00:55:25.955
Do you want to
jump in here next?

00:55:25.955 --> 00:55:26.720
TREVOR: Yeah.

00:55:26.720 --> 00:55:28.490
Hi, Rachel, Hi, everyone.

00:55:28.490 --> 00:55:30.230
Hi, Josh.

00:55:30.230 --> 00:55:33.570
So, my question-- I'd made a
statement before in the chat,

00:55:33.570 --> 00:55:38.900
but I'm turning it
into a question, right?

00:55:38.900 --> 00:55:42.830
So, last week, I was asking
about the impact of AI

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:44.270
on data science, right?

00:55:44.270 --> 00:55:50.780
So I'm continuing in that vein
where, do you think, Josh--

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:52.770
and it's just your opinion.

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:57.050
Given the advance of
AI, and we clearly

00:55:57.050 --> 00:55:59.980
see AI is making great strides
in terms of writing codes,

00:55:59.980 --> 00:56:00.480
right?

00:56:00.480 --> 00:56:01.640
Writing code.

00:56:01.640 --> 00:56:06.460
Do you think that
most for our time

00:56:06.460 --> 00:56:11.440
will be spent more
on qualitative things

00:56:11.440 --> 00:56:17.920
such as assisting stakeholders
in defining the problem as

00:56:17.920 --> 00:56:23.260
opposed to thinking about
the latest way to code

00:56:23.260 --> 00:56:27.060
or thinking about the
latest algorithm or anything

00:56:27.060 --> 00:56:27.560
like that?

00:56:27.560 --> 00:56:29.470
Do you see, like, a
shift or a balance

00:56:29.470 --> 00:56:32.170
or a shift in terms of
where we spend our time

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:34.000
as data scientists?

00:56:34.000 --> 00:56:36.550
Yeah, that's a really
engaging question.

00:56:36.550 --> 00:56:38.960
I think there's a lot
of hot takes on that.

00:56:38.960 --> 00:56:41.703
I think for the immediate
future, if nothing else,

00:56:41.703 --> 00:56:43.870
it's going to allow data
scientists to probably code

00:56:43.870 --> 00:56:45.070
a bit more efficiently.

00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:47.840
I think a significant
challenge--

00:56:47.840 --> 00:56:50.050
and, like, I haven't asked,
like, ChatGPT to go in

00:56:50.050 --> 00:56:52.160
and build something, write
a piece of code for me.

00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:53.618
I think my wife is
done this, which

00:56:53.618 --> 00:56:59.920
maybe alleviates my requirement
to utilize helper for her.

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:01.420
But along those
lines, I think it's

00:57:01.420 --> 00:57:03.220
going to allow data scientists
to be more efficient as they're

00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:03.790
coding.

00:57:03.790 --> 00:57:06.460
I'd say it's probably
like five to seven years

00:57:06.460 --> 00:57:08.950
until I can actually do
anything substantially

00:57:08.950 --> 00:57:12.820
relevant to consider, like,
data scientists are only working

00:57:12.820 --> 00:57:16.150
on framing problems in a way
